🖥️ What Is a Command-Line Application?
A command-line application is a program you interact with using a terminal (also called the command line or shell) — without clicking or using a graphical interface. You type in commands to tell the computer what to do.

Example:
Typing this in the terminal:

bash
Copy
Edit
gobuster -u http://fakebank.thm -w wordlist.txt dir
...will start Gobuster and scan for hidden pages on a website.

🧰 What Is Gobuster?
Gobuster is a command-line tool used for:

Brute-forcing URLs (directories or pages) on websites.

Finding hidden content like /admin, /secret, /login that is not visible on the main site.

🧪 The Gobuster Command Explained
bash
Copy
Edit
gobuster -u http://fakebank.thm -w wordlist.txt dir
🧩 Breakdown:
Part	Meaning
gobuster	Starts the tool
-u	(URL) The website you want to scan (e.g., http://fakebank.thm)
-w	(Wordlist) A file with possible directory or page names (e.g., wordlist.txt)
dir	Tells Gobuster to search for directories (not DNS or files)

🧠 What Happens When You Run It?
Gobuster:

Takes every word in wordlist.txt

Tries to open http://fakebank.thm/<word> (like /admin, /bank-transfer)

Shows the status code:

200 – Page exists ✅

301 – Redirected

403 – Exists but forbidden

404 – Not shown (means page doesn't exist)

